 o(s,v+1)-o(s,v)&=-s\delta,\\
 \sigma(s,v+1)-\sigma(s,v)&=-s\delta+\gamma(s,v),\\
 o(s+4,v)-o(s,v)&=-\gamma(s,v)-2\delta,\\
 \sigma(s+4,v)-\sigma(s,v)&=0.
 \end{aligned}                                   \tag{P3F.23.4}
\] Each discrepancy beyond \(P3F.23.2\) is an actual integral lattice period. Hence these origins descend to sections of the specified torus torsor, with the cyclic cusp cover retained. The cusp origin \(\sigma\) is holomorphic and bounded in \(q\) for each \(r\ne0\); its characters act by units in the cusp toric charts, so it extends there. Subtracting \(o\) removes the parameter deck. Over any fixed nonzero \(q\), the remaining period multiplier is \(e^{2\pi iC}q r^4\), and the standard four-node rank-one chain \(X_i=V/r^i,\ Y_i=r^{i+1}/V,\ X_iY_i=r\) supplies the partial parameter filling. Its zero-coordinate origin extends. Thus these are the extending origins for the two actual collars, not arbitrary polynomials satisfying only a finite-difference identity.

\item Their difference is \[
 F=\sigma-o=
 \binom{\Gamma(s+4v)/4}
       {C(s+4v)/4+(s+4v)^2/8-2v}.
\] Direct subtraction proves \(P3F.23.1\). The additional real delta coefficient vanishes at \(\theta=0,1\), so its exponentiation is a periodic unitary gauge homotopic to the constant gauge through periodic gauges. It vanishes in the circle quotient. Furthermore \[
 \begin{split}
 &[F(s+4,v+\theta)-F(s+4,v)]
       -[F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)]\\
 &\hspace{35mm}=4\theta\delta.
 \end{split}
\] The corresponding transition is \(e^{8\pi i\theta}\), with winding four. This is the retained line clutch for the actual positive parameter loop. The full ordinary and cusp origins, all four deck equations, the angular difference and both endpoint values are checked symbolically in \path{code/current_audits/audit_corrections/code/independent_IV_classical_inputs.py}; its exact results are saved in \path{code/current_audits/audit_corrections/code/independent_IV_classical_inputs.json}.

\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

